1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. When you push one agent to handle a big, messy job end-to-end,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
its context balloons and its answers get worse. A manager that delegates scoped pieces to subagents

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
can fix that problem. This lesson covers how a manager decomposes work and orchestrates the

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
results. Let's jump into it. Three roles anchor this pattern. The manager is the orchestrator.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
It owns the goal, breaks it into pieces, hands each piece off, and stitches the answers together.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
A subagent is a separate agent instance the manager spawns to handle one focused subtask,

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
and it reports a single result back. Delegation is the act of handing that scoped subtask off,

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
and in the agent SDK it happens through the agent tool. The model itself decides when spinning up

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
a subagent is worth it based on how you described each one. Keep these three straight because the

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
rest of the pattern builds directly on them. So what does the manager actually do? It runs

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
the same loop on every job. First it decomposes the goal, breaking one large request into

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
independent subtasks that can each be handled on their own. Then it delegates, handing each

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
subtask to a subagent chosen for that piece of work. Finally it synthesizes, pulling the

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
returned results together into a single coherent answer for the user. Notice what the manager

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
doesn't do. It doesn't carry out the subtask itself. Its job is coordination, deciding what to

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
split off, who gets what, and how the pieces fit back together. That division of labor is the whole

17
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
point of the pattern. Here's a distinction that trips people up. Orchestrator workers versus

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
parallelization. They look almost identical on a diagram. One coordinator fanning work out to

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
several agents. The difference is who decides the subtasks. With parallelization, you define the

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
subtasks in your own code ahead of time. You already know the independent sections, so you split them

21
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
and run them together. With orchestrator workers, the subtasks aren't predefined. The manager looks

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
at the specific input and decides at runtime how to break the job apart. That runtime flexibility

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
is the whole reason to reach for orchestration. If the split is always the same, you don't need

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
a manager deciding it. Plain parallel code is simpler and cheaper. How does delegation actually

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
happen in the SDK? The manager calls a built-in tool named agent, and that tool is what spins up

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
a sub-agent. Each sub-agent you define carries a description, a short statement of what it's good

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
for. When the manager hits a subtask, Claude matches that description against the work in front of it

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
and picks the sub-agent that fits. You aren't forced to leave it to the model, though. You can

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
also name a sub-agent directly in the prompt when you want a specific one. One practical detail

30
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
include the agent tool in your allowed tools list, so these delegations get auto-approved

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
instead of pausing for a permission prompt every time. That approves the delegation itself. The

32
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
tools a sub-agent runs still go through permission separately, so pre-approve those with permission

33
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
rules or a pre-tool use hook. Why bother splitting work into sub-agents at all? Three payoffs and the

34
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:31,000
slide names them. Context isolation, parallelization, and tool restriction. Isolation is the deep one,

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and it gets a full lesson of its own next, so for now hold just the headline. Whatever a sub-agent

36
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
does internally stays out of the manager's window. What matters here is what those payoffs demand

37
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
from your delegation mechanics. Because each sub-agent runs apart from the manager, the handoff

38
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,000
has to carry everything the sub-task needs, and the tool list you attach is what draws that

39
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
sub-agent's lane. Scope the handoff and the tools well, and all three payoffs follow from the same

40
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,000
discipline. A lot of delegation quality is decided before a single sub-agent runs in how you scope

41
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
the work. Start with scope. Give each sub-agent one clear job, not a vague region of the problem.

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
A sub-task with a fuzzy boundary hands you back a fuzzy result you can't trust.

43
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Next, be explicit about the output format you expect, whether that's a short summary, a list,

44
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
or structured data. The sub-agent can only match a shape you've named. Then, back at the manager,

45
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
don't just accept what returns. Collect every result and validate it before you synthesize.

46
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
A sub-agent can fail or drift, and the manager is the one place you catch that before it reaches

47
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:50,000
the user. Three guardrails keep delegation safe. The first is least privilege. Give each sub-agent

48
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
only the tools and permissions its task actually requires, so it simply can't reach outside its

49
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
lane, even if something goes sideways. The second is the side effect boundary. A well-scoped

50
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,000
sub-agent should produce no writes and no actions beyond its assigned sub-task. If a summarizer

51
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
starts deleting files, the scope was wrong. The third is result validation. The manager checks

52
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
each returned result before folding it into the final answer, because a sub-agent can return

53
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
something plausible, but wrong. Notice these are the manager's job. The sub-agent stays narrow

54
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:33,000
on purpose, and the manager holds the authority and the final check. Let's close the loop on where

55
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
developers go wrong. Three anti-patterns show up again and again. The first is overly broad scope.

56
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
You hand a sub-agent a job that's really five jobs, and it wanders, burns tokens, and comes back with

57
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
something muddy. Keep each sub-agent narrow. The second is underpowered context. Because a sub-agent

58
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
starts fresh, it only knows what you hand it. Give it too little, and it fails a task it could easily

59
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
have done. The third is treating sub-agents as nothing but parallel workers. Speed is a nice bonus,

60
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
but the deeper win is context isolation, keeping the manager's window clean. Miss that, and you've

61
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:19,000
turned a strong pattern into an unreliable one. Let's recap. A manager breaks a goal into scoped

62
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:26,000
sub-tasks, delegates each to a sub-agent, and synthesizes the results. It matters because it

63
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:33,000
keeps one agent from drowning in a job too big for it. Just remember, orchestrator workers decide

64
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
the split at runtime, which is what sets them apart from fixed parallel code. That covers how

65
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
a manager delegates work to sub-agents. I'll see you in the next one.

